### Express Error Handling and Middleware

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Covers improvements and fixes related to error handling, middleware precedence, and the definition of error handlers within Express.js applications.

```APIDOC
Error handler definition:
  - Fixed issue preventing error handlers from being defined anywhere.

Middleware precedence:
  - Fixed middleware/router precedence issues.

Error propagation:
  - Fixed `res.sendfile()` error handling, deferring via `next()`.
  - Fixed `res.render()` exceptions, now passed to `next()` when no callback is given.
```

--------------------------------

### Wrapping of rejected promise in handleError (error middleware layer)

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/node_modules/router/lib/layer.js

Same wrapping behavior applies to error middleware: if an error handler's returned promise rejects with no value, it becomes `new Error('Rejected promise')` forwarded to the next error handler.

```javascript
Layer.prototype.handleError = function handleError (error, req, res, next) {
  const fn = this.handle

  if (fn.length !== 4) {
    // not a standard error handler
    return next(error)
  }

  try {
    // invoke function
    const ret = fn(error, req, res, next)

    // wait for returned promise
    if (isPromise(ret)) {
      if (!(ret instanceof Promise)) {
        deprecate('handlers that are Promise-like are deprecated, use a native Promise instead')
      }

      ret.then(null, function (error) {
        next(error || new Error('Rejected promise'))
      })
    }
  } catch (err) {
    next(err)
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Custom JSON 404 middleware

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/master/examples/web-service/index.js

Placed last, this 404 handler returns a JSON error response when no other middleware matched the route. This is the simplest and most direct pattern for a custom 404 in Express.

```javascript
// our custom JSON 404 middleware. Since it's placed last
// it will be the last middleware called, if all others
// invoke next() and do not respond.
app.use(function(req, res){
  res.status(404);
  res.send({ error: "Sorry, can't find that" })
});
```

--------------------------------

### Express.js: Handling Client Aborts in sendfile/sendFile

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Ensures that the callback function for `res.sendFile`, `res.sendfile`, and `res.download` is invoked when the client aborts the request. The `err` object will be populated with the request aborted error.

```JavaScript
res.sendFile('/path/to/large/file', (err) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('File transfer aborted by client:', err);
  }
});
```

--------------------------------

### Use http-errors for res.format errors

Source: https://github.com/expressjs/express/blob/v5.1.0/History.md

Integrates the `http-errors` library for generating errors within `res.format`. This provides standardized and informative error objects for better error handling.

```javascript
res.format({
  'text/plain': function() { /* ... */ },
  'application/json': function() { /* ... */ },
  'default': function() {
    // Use http-errors for consistent error responses
    const err = createError(406, 'Not Acceptable');
    throw err;
  }
});
```
